{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>From the fruit of a man's mouth shall his belly be satisfied; with the produce of his lips shall he be sated (Proverbs 18:20).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, of blessed memory, informs us in this verse (Proverbs 18) of the great power of the tongue, whether for good or for evil. For if a man uses his tongue in words of Torah and reproves and merits the many, behold, his reward is with him and his labor is before Him; but if in words of talebearing and evil speech, behold, his punishment is ready and prepared, and his belly shall be sated from it. And the beginning of the verse, ״מפרי פי איש תשבע בטנו״ — 'from the fruit of a man's mouth shall his belly be satisfied' — concerns the punishment for evil speech, while its conclusion, when it says ״תבואת שפתיו ישבע״ — 'with the produce of his lips shall he be sated' — concerns the reward of the righteous man who vindicates and merits the many with his tongue. And the verse that follows it is bound to it and explains it, namely (ibid.): ״מות וחיים ביד לשון ואוהביה יאכל פריה״ — 'Death and life are in the hand of the tongue, and those who love it shall eat its fruit.' It means to say: since there are death and life in the hand of the tongue, whoever loves the tongue — that is, one who is eager to speak constantly — should strive to increase his reward and speak in matters of wisdom and reproofs of life and truth and peace, and he shall eat the fruit of the tongue and his reward shall be great, for life is in the hand of the tongue, and just as he is habituated to speak more than other men, so shall his reward be multiplied. And in the opposite manner as well: if one loves the tongue for words of talebearing and evil speech, surely his punishment is great, and he shall eat the fruit of the punishment; therefore one must take care in this, for death and life are in the hand of the tongue. And the Torah informs us of the punishment for evil speech: whoever does not guard himself against it is punished and stricken with tzara'at; and from this he is called by the name מצורע, the explanation of which is מוציא שם רע — 'one who brings forth an evil name' — for the name of the punishment explains the sin for which it is inflicted. And the verse requires of him a sacrifice to atone for the sin of the tongue, and he has no purification except through a priest, and this is what is written." "<b>This shall be the Torah of the metzora on the day of his purification, and he shall be brought to the priest.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory, the masters of truth, expounded: afflictions come only because of evil speech, as it is said (Ecclesiastes 5): ״אל תתן את פיך לחטיא את בשרך״ — 'Do not let your mouth cause your flesh to sin.' That is to say, do not give permission to bring forth any word from your mouth to cause your flesh to sin, to afflict your body. And do not say before the angel appointed over you, 'I uttered the word inadvertently'; for every single word that goes forth from your mouth is written in a book, whether good or evil, whether unintentional or deliberate — ״למה יקצוף האלהים על קולך וחבל את מעשה ידיך״ — 'Why should Elohim be angry at your voice and destroy the work of your hands?' — these are the hands and the body that are afflicted with afflictions. Another interpretation: ״אל תתן את פיך״ — the Torah spoke in euphemistic language: if your wife tells you that she is in a state of menstrual impurity (niddah), do not cause your body to sin and force yourself upon her; and do not say before the angel appointed over the form of the offspring, 'I was inadvertent; I did not know' — ״למה יקצוף״ etc. — ״וחבל את מעשה ידיך״ — these are the children who are afflicted with afflictions. Another interpretation: ״אל תתן את פיך לחטיא את בשרך״ — if you sinned deliberately, you will need a priest, who is called an angel, as it is said (Malachi 2): ״כי שפתי כהן ישמרו דעת ותורה יבקשו מפיהו כי מלאך ה' צבאות הוא״ — 'For the lips of a priest guard knowledge, and they seek Torah from his mouth, for he is an angel of YHWH of Hosts.' And do not say before the priest, 'I sinned inadvertently' — you deceive yourself; ״למה יקצוף האלהים״ etc. — ״מעשה ידיך״ — these are the children who are afflicted with tzara'at." "<b>And</b> what it says, 'on the day of his purification' — on the day that he desires to become purified — 'and he shall be brought to the priest' — this teaches you that he never has purification except by the word of a priest; for the matter is not that the metzora comes to the priest, but rather the priest would go out to him, outside the camp, to where he is. This is what it mentions: 'and the priest shall go out outside the camp.'" "<b>And they said</b> in the Midrash: ״מות וחיים ביד לשון״ (Proverbs 18:21) — everything depends on the tongue. If one is meritorious, he gains life; if not, death. If a man engages in Torah with his tongue, he merits life — and that is the Torah, which is called a tree of life, as it is said (ibid.): ״עץ חיים היא למחזיקים בה״ — 'She is a tree of life to those who hold her.' And it is the remedy for evil speech, as it is said (Proverbs 15): ״מרפא לשון עץ חיים״ — 'A healing tongue is a tree of life.' If one engages in evil speech, he is liable to death, for evil speech is more severe than bloodshed: for whoever kills kills only one soul, but the one who speaks evil speech kills three — the one who says it, the one who receives it, and the one about whom it is said. The one who says it: from Doeg the Edomite, who spoke evil speech against Ahimelech son of Ahitub, and he was killed, as it is said (1 Samuel 22): ״ויאמר שאול מות תמות אחימלך״ — 'And Saul said, You shall die, Ahimelech.' And Saul was killed, as it is written (1 Chronicles 10): ״וימת שאול במעליו אשר מעל באלהים״ — 'And Saul died for his treachery by which he acted treacherously against Elohim.' And likewise Saul said (2 Samuel 1): ״עמד נא עלי ומותתני כי אחזני השבץ״ — 'Stand over me and kill me, for the shavetz has seized me' — the indictment of Nov, the city of the priests; and shavetz is none other than the priestly garments, as it is said (Exodus 28): ״ועשית משבצות זהב״ — 'And you shall make settings of gold.' And Doeg the Edomite was uprooted from the life of this world and from the life of the world to come, as it is said (Psalms 52): ״גם אל יתצך לנצח יחתך ויסחך מאהל ושרשך מארץ חיים סלה״ — 'Elohim will likewise break you forever; He will take you up and pluck you out of your tent, and uproot you from the land of the living, Selah.' Which is more severe: one who strikes with a sword or one who strikes with an arrow? One who strikes with a sword can kill only at close range, but one who strikes with an arrow is not so — rather he shoots the arrow and strikes him wherever he sees him. And therefore one who speaks evil speech is likened to an arrow, as it is said (Jeremiah 9): ״חץ שחוט לשונם מרמה דבר בפיו שלום את רעהו ידבר ובקרבו ישים ארבו״ — 'Their tongue is a sharpened arrow; it speaks deceit. With his mouth one speaks peace to his neighbor, but in his heart he lays an ambush.' And likewise the verse says (Psalms 57): ״בני אדם שניהם חנית וחצים ולשונם חרב חדה״ — 'The sons of men, whose teeth are spears and arrows, and their tongue a sharp sword.'" "<b>Evil speech (lashon ha-ra) is harsher</b> than bloodshed and forbidden sexual relations and idolatry — than bloodshed, as it is written (Genesis 4:13): ״ויאמר קין אל ה' גדול עוני מנשוא״; than forbidden sexual relations, as it is written (Genesis 39:9): ״ואיך אעשה הרעה הגדולה הזאת״; than idolatry, as it is written (Exodus 32:31): ״אנא חטא העם הזה חטאה גדולה״; whereas concerning evil speech it is written (Psalms 12:4): ״יכרת ה' כל שפתי חלקות לשון מדברת גדולות״; therefore it is said: 'Death and life are in the hand of the tongue.' <br><b>In the case of Miriam</b> (Numbers 12:1): ״ותדבר מרים ואהרן במשה״ — what was their punishment? Leprosy (tzara'at), as it is written (there): ״ויפן אהרן אל מרים והנה מצורעת״, and it is written (there): ״ויחר אף ה' בם וילך״ — Aaron is included. What is written (Deuteronomy 24:9): ״זכור את אשר עשה ה' אלהיך למרים בדרך״ — and is this not an a fortiori argument (קל וחומר)? And if Miriam, who spoke only about her brother, and not in his presence, and did not intend anything but to restore him to his wife — was so punished, then one who speaks lashon ha-ra in the presence of his fellow about another, all the more so! What is written above concerning the matter: 'Take heed regarding the plague of leprosy.' And even Aaron, who was a priest, the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, touched him, as it is said: ״ויחר אף ה' בם וילך״ — 'in them' means in Aaron and Miriam; only that Aaron was healed immediately, and Miriam after seven days, as it is said (Numbers 12:15): ״ותסגר מרים מחוץ למחנה שבעת ימים״. <br><b>And likewise</b> you find in the primordial serpent: because it spoke lashon ha-ra about its Creator, it was therefore stricken with leprosy, as it is said (Genesis 3:14): ״ויאמר ה' אלקים אל הנחש כי עשית זאת ארור אתה״ — cursed with leprosy, as it is written (Leviticus 13:52): ״צרעת ממארת היא״. R. Huna said in the name of R. Yehoshua ben Levi: the scales that are upon the serpent — that is its leprosy. And not only that, but blemished persons are healed in the world to come, while it is not healed like the children of men, as it is said (Isaiah 35:6): ״אז ידלג כאיל פסח ותרון לשון אלם״; and likewise beasts and animals — 'and wolf and lamb shall graze together' — but the serpent: 'dust shall be its bread,' for it is never healed, because it brought creatures down to the dust. And what caused it? That it spoke lashon ha-ra."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {5 "The opening lemma (קשה לשון הרע) is rendered as a comparative: lashon ha-ra is 'harsher' than the three cardinal sins, per the prooftext chain that follows. The scriptural quotation 'מות וחיים ביד לשון' (Proverbs 18:21) is cited without a verse reference in the source; reference supplied for clarity."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:54.973791934Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}